Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["                              TRACING", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.455193478921875*^9, 3.455193498328125*^9}, 
   3.45519356775*^9, {3.970029206629711*^9, 3.970029213032406*^9}, 
   3.9790906529765205`*^9, {3.9793461769917126`*^9, 3.9793461785837092`*^9}},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.8392156862745098, 0.8392156862745098, 
   0.8392156862745098],ExpressionUUID->"0cf1fe24-6765-8749-bd8e-5c3febfd3df0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Before loading the notebook Tracing, the user must to load \
Taylor-Implicit and TotalAberrations.\n\nThe program Tracing has the \
following input data :\nrad = list of radii\nthick = list of distances \
between the  surfaces of the dioptrics along the optical axis\nind = the list \
of refractive indices between the surfaces\ncostasf = aspheric constants of \
surfaces\nstoprad = radius of the stop\nnstop = number of the surfaces before \
the stop\ndis = distance of the stop from the last surface before the stop\n\
dobject = distance of the object from the first surface (negative)\nhobject = \
height of the object from the optical axis\nwaves = wave length\n\nThe \
program evaluates the path of a given set of rays entering the entrance pupil \
and evaluates by a ray tracing the third-order and fifth-order aberrations. \n\
\nWhen the object is at infinity, we could  adopt  ",
  FontSize->16],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"dobject", "=", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", "15"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "140dca6b-2582-254c-ae62-fce15344e13e"],
 StyleBox[" and ",
  FontSize->16],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"hobject", "=", 
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["10", "15"], 
      RowBox[{"Tan", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\[Alpha]", 
        FractionBox["\[Pi]", "180"]}], "]"}]}]}], ",", " ", "where", "  "}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"19358a5c-0a4a-3e40-920b-849f8bab3378"],
 StyleBox["\[Alpha] is the field angle  in degrees. \n\nThe results could \
improve considerably by expressing the dimensions of the optical system in \
centimeters or decimeters instead of millimeters. However, the program \
provides the aberrations, initially evaluated in centimeters or decimeters, \
in millimeters, by multiplying the results obtained by fact = 10 or 100. It \
is very important to note that the conic constants, which may characterize \
some surfaces, must not be modified in this change of dimensions. In \
contrast, the fourth-order aspheric constant must be multiplied by (10",
  FontSize->16],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["", 
    RowBox[{"-", "3"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f563046a-5625-9c4d-8e38-ee4935bb6039"],
 StyleBox[") when fact = 10, and by ",
  FontSize->16],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "6"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "37302d8c-8ecc-c841-8fa6-91d584bcfd88"],
 StyleBox[" when fact = 100.\nIn the examples that follow, the \
above-mentioned logic has been adopted, and the results obtained can be \
compared with those of OSLO by using the example file contained in ExsOSLO.",
  FontSize->16]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.45519372409375*^9, 3.455193838375*^9}, {
